3.3.2 Logic and Set Theory
The basic logical operations can be typeset using rather self-explanatory commands: \lnot, \land, \lor, \implies and \iff.
\[ p \land q \iff \lnot (p \implies \lnot q) \]
p ∧ q ⟺ ¬(p ⟹ ¬q)
There’s also a left facing implication named \impliedBy. Some less established logical symbols can also be found under less intuitive names such as \veebar, which produces ⊻ used by some for exclusive disjunction. If you intend to use them, make sure to use commands described in Section 2.9 to create meaningful synonyms. The quantifier symbols can be typeset using \forall and \exists commands.
\[ \lnot\forall_{q} P(q) \iff \exists_{q}\lnot P(q) \]
